# Summary
Introduced a memory-safety/UB risk in the new PCLMUL CRC64 loop by performing out-of-bounds pointer arithmetic in the loop condition.
The added PCLMUL-accelerated CRC64 implementation uses `while ptr.add(128) <= end` to decide whether another 128‑byte block remains. When `ptr` is already equal to `end` (e.g., data lengths of exactly 128 bytes or multiples of 128), evaluating `ptr.add(128)` computes a pointer 128 bytes past the one‑past‑end boundary, which is undefined behavior in Rust even if it is not dereferenced. This is reachable with attacker‑controlled object sizes on x86_64 builds that enable the `pure-rust` feature and run on CPUs with `pclmulqdq`, potentially leading to crashes or miscompilation in the checksum path.

Fixed in commit `99c70c1` (`Fix CRC64 pclmul pointer arithmetic UB`).
The loop now tracks remaining bytes explicitly instead of probing
`ptr.add(128)` in the loop condition, so it no longer forms out-of-bounds
raw pointers during block iteration.

