## Report
Rubric-driven steps: (1) Built and ran a minimal PoC to hit the pclmul path on 128/256-byte inputs: `cargo run -p checksum --bin poc_crc64 --no-default-features --features pure-rust` produced `crc128=0xa5296136625de4f7` and `crc256=0xff71e21279d9966e` with no crash. (2) Attempted ASan: `RUSTFLAGS='-Zsanitizer=address' cargo run ...` failed because the available toolchains are stable only (error: option `Z` only accepted on nightly). (3) Valgrind not available (`valgrind: command not found`). (4) Debugger attempt with lldb ran the PoC without a crash (see artifacts/lldb_run.txt). (5) Code inspection confirms UB: in `extend_blocks_only` the loop condition `while ptr.add(128) <= end` computes `ptr.add(128)` even when `ptr == end`, which is 128 bytes past the one-past-end boundary, violating Rust’s pointer arithmetic rules even without dereference (crates/checksum/src/crc64.rs:242-270). This path is reachable on x86_64 with `pure-rust` + runtime `pclmulqdq` detection (`extend` chooses this path; crates/checksum/src/crc64.rs:230-239). Thus the reported UB risk is valid though it did not crash in this environment.

