# Attack-path analysis
Final: low | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
Original severity assumed a broadly reachable runtime issue. In practice, the UB is confined to the opt-in pure-rust build (README notes test-only), requires x86_64 pclmul, and no crash was demonstrated; the plausible impact is availability-only. These constraints reduce likelihood and impact to low.
## Likelihood
low - Requires a non-default pure-rust build and x86_64 pclmul hardware; deployments are described as test-only. Even then, only a potential UB/DoS was demonstrated without a crash.
## Impact
low - Undefined behavior in checksum computation could lead to crashes or miscompilation, primarily affecting availability; no crash was observed in validation and the path is limited to non-default builds.
## Assumptions
- Deployment is built with the non-default pure-rust CRC feature (e.g., --no-default-features --features pure-rust or pure-rust-crc).
- The server runs on x86_64 hardware with pclmulqdq available so the PCLMUL fast path is selected.
- Attacker can trigger CRC64 on object data by uploading/reading objects (authenticated or via public bucket ACL).
- Build with pure-rust CRC feature enabled (non-default)
- x86_64 CPU with pclmulqdq feature detected
- CRC64 invoked on data length >=128 and multiple of 128
- Attacker can read/write object data (auth or public bucket)
## Path
HTTP client -> ReadHandle::next_chunk/crc64.update -> crc64::extend_blocks_only (pclmul) -> UB/DoS
## Path evidence
- `crates/checksum/src/crc64.rs:242-270` - PCLMUL loop uses `while ptr.add(128) <= end`, which computes an out-of-bounds pointer when ptr == end.
- `crates/checksum/src/crc64.rs:80-87` - The PCLMUL fast path is only used in the pure-rust build on x86_64 with pclmulqdq detected.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:1600-1609` - Object reads stream data chunks and call crc64.update on bytes, making checksum reachable in runtime data paths.
- `README.md:33-57` - Pure-rust build is described as test-only/non-production, reducing real-world exposure.
- `crates/server-core/Cargo.toml:19-24` - Default features use ISA-L CRC; pure-rust CRC is an opt-in feature.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:27-63` - Shows default listen address (127.0.0.1:9000) and env-sourced secrets/credentials.
## Narrative
The PCLMUL-accelerated CRC64 loop computes ptr.add(128) in the loop condition even when ptr == end, which is undefined behavior in Rust. This path is only selected when the pure-rust feature is enabled and the CPU supports pclmulqdq. The server’s runtime streams object data and updates a CRC64 hasher over attacker-controlled bytes, so the UB is reachable with object sizes that yield chunk lengths that are multiples of 128. The README explicitly labels pure-rust builds as test-only/non-production, and the provided validation PoC did not trigger a crash, so the practical impact is limited to potential availability issues in non-default builds.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication for private buckets
- Configurable max_connections/max_inflight_requests
- Optional TLS when cert/key env vars are set
## Blindspots
- No runtime sanitizer or crash evidence in this environment; UB impact remains theoretical.
- No deployment manifests/IaC to confirm whether pure-rust builds are used in production or exposed publicly.
